{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Treat node1's /etc/inventory/app.conf as authoritative and maintain a one-way synchronized copy on node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A source-only change left node2 temporarily stale, then the synchronization job copied node1's content atomically to node2 with matching SHA-256 and content. Source-to-target parity was also demonstrated after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b1c7a00b95674f49","cmd-24b55863871041e4","cmd-782a2bd42d2846c1","cmd-056a5352c7884fc3","cmd-93353e056ae04b26"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Automatically pick up changes from node1 on node2 within 15 minutes.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Node2 had a running, boot-enabled crond service and a */15 schedule invoking the successfully tested synchronization job. However, the captured change was picked up by manually invoking that job six seconds later; no evidence directly observes crond triggering the job autonomously at a scheduled boundary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9adcb8ca52404ba1","cmd-b1c7a00b95674f49","cmd-24b55863871041e4","cmd-782a2bd42d2846c1","cmd-507e8c76cfa04129","cmd-93353e056ae04b26"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"Task-related mutations had clear roles: creating the initially absent source, provisioning dedicated access, installing and enabling the synchronization job, making a bounded verification change, and performing the mandatory restart checks. The only unnecessary retained mutations were /tmp/inventory-reboot.log files left on node1 and node2 by the reboot scheduling commands; neither was cleaned up. Package-health output was unchanged and post-task socket observations showed no new listening service class.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4739d7867d1041b0","cmd-a9f57bd2738d4840","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:before_prepare:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:before_prepare:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"The one-way synchronization mechanism works and remained healthy across sequential restarts, but the strict 15-minute automatic-pickup outcome is only partially proven because the tested source change was followed by a manual job invocation rather than an observed cron-triggered run. The provider-wide restart protocol is otherwise strong for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered services, data, schedule, and source/target parity.","limitations":["No captured command observes crond itself launching the synchronization job after a source change, so autonomous pickup within the promised interval remains indirectly supported by configuration.","Alpine lacks the lifecycle collector's systemd failed-unit check; service recovery was instead directly checked with OpenRC commands.","The fixed lifecycle snapshots do not inventory the task-specific configuration files or accounts, so their persistence is established primarily by executor commands."]}
